WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.740
Welcome back to unintended consequences, episode 46, its July 2nd, 2026.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:13.080
Every story we cover starts with someone who had a reasonable plan. On October 24th, 1978,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.340
President Jimmy Carter signed the Airline D-Regulation Act in the White House Rose Garden.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:24.600
Economists who had argued for years that regulated fares kept ordinary Americans on the ground,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.980
stood beside him. The law removed the civil aeronautics

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:35.620
boards power to set prices and approve routes. The explicit goal was to let competition drive

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:42.840
costs down. Within two decades, the average fare had fallen sharply. Yet dozens of smaller cities

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:49.380
had lost all scheduled service. The workforce that once flew those planes saw real wages decline

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:57.220
for the first time since the jet age began. By the late 2010s, American, Delta, United, and

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:03.600
Southwest controlled roughly 80% of domestic passengers. Hub and spoke networks and economies

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:10.040
of scale rewarded the largest carriers. More than 100 airlines exited through bankruptcy or

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:15.920
merger. The same freedom that lowered fares on busy routes also rewarded carriers that could

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:22.280
spread fixed costs across larger networks. Airlines quickly adopted the hub and spoke model,

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:28.460
concentrating flights at a handful of major airports. This structure produced powerful economies

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:35.040
of scale that favored the largest carriers. The result was lower fares on high-volume routes alongside

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:41.460
thinner service and higher effective costs elsewhere. Dozens of smaller cities lost all scheduled

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:48.540
service after 1983. Carriers were now free to drop low-density routes. Congress created the

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:55.960
Essential Air Service subsidy in 1978. That program now supports only a fraction of 1978 routes

00:01:55.960 --> 00:02:03.180
at roughly $300 million a year. The gap between promised universal access and actual connectivity

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:09.360
became permanent. Real compensation for pilots and flight attendants declined for the first time

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.820
in the jet age. Carriers shifted flying to lower-cost regional partners. Legacy carriers

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:21.860
negotiated successive wage concessions under relentless cost pressure. Productivity gains

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:28.200
flowed to consumers on dense routes rather than to the workforce. The regulated system that existed

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:36.000
from 1938 to 1978 had produced stable but expensive service. The Civil Aeronautics Board treated

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.040
airlines like public utilities. It approved fares that guaranteed carriers a reasonable return

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:49.160
while preventing price wars. By the mid-1970s inflation had pushed coach fares on many routes above

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:56.400
what middle-income families could easily pay. Alfred E. Conn, an economist appointed to lead the

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:03.100
cab in 1977 and Senator Edward Kennedy believed that removing government price controls would allow

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.940
new entrants to compete on cost and route structure. Their reasoning rested on straightforward

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:14.580
micro-economics. If carriers could charge whatever the market would bear and fly whatever cities

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:21.680
proved profitable, fares would fall and capacity would rise. At the time, no one had seen a fully

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.660
deregulated national passenger network in the jet era. The experiment felt like a low-risk

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:34.620
correction of an obvious inefficiency. The Airline D-Regulation Act phased out the CAB's authority

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:41.300
over domestic fares and routes by 1983. It dissolved the agency itself in 1985.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:49.340
Early results appeared encouraging. Between 1978 and 1985, real average fares dropped roughly

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:58.100
20%. Passenger implants rose from 240 million to more than 340 million. Low-cost carriers such as

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:03.480
people express and New York air entered the market with dramatically lower prices on high-density

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:09.520
corridors. Proponents pointed to these numbers as proof that the policy was working as intended.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:15.180
A few skeptics, including some airline executives and labor leaders, warned that smaller

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:20.020
communities would lose service once carriers were free to abandon unprofitable routes.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.720
Those concerns received limited attention during the first years of expansion.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:29.760
Lawmakers responded with targeted interventions rather than re-regulation.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:36.400
The Essential Air Service Subsidy preserves service to about 60 small airports at an annual cost

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:44.780
of roughly $300 million. Antitrust authorities blocked some proposed mergers in the 1980s and 1990s.

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.320
They approved the largest combinations after 2008, including the United Continental and American

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:58.820
US Airways deals. No serious legislative effort has emerged to restore broad fare or route regulation.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:04.520
The current industry structure reflects four decades of adaptation to the incentives created

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:11.780
in 1978. When policy makers remove constraints on pricing and entry, the resulting market tends

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:18.420
to reward scale and network effects more quickly than regulators anticipate. Incentive structures that

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:23.960
appear neutral on paper often amplify existing differences in cost structure and geographic

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:30.620
position. Before changing the rules that govern an entire industry, it is useful to model not only

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:35.300
the first round price effects, but also the second round consolidation that can follow.

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.820
Once firms are free to choose which customers they will serve.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:45.120
The airline case continues to shape debates over whether similar deregulation in other network

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:50.860
industries will produce comparable concentration. Consider current proposals to ease entry

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:56.540
and pricing rules in broadband or freight rail. What second round ownership patterns would you

00:05:56.540 --> 00:06:02.020
watch for once the largest players can select their customers without constraint? We'll leave it

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:07.180
there for today. Thanks for thinking through this one with me. If you know someone who loves a good

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:12.960
cautionary tale, send it their way, and I'll see you tomorrow with another. And if you'd rather watch

